\begin{proof}
\textbf{Construction of a quadratic function.}
For an arbitrary real parameter \(t\) define
\[
f(t)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}(a_i-t\,b_i)^{2}.
\]
Each summand is a square of a real number; hence
\[
f(t)\ge 0\qquad\text{for all }t\in\mathbb{R}. \tag{1}
\]

\textbf{Expansion.}
For a single term,
\[
(a_i-tb_i)^{2}=a_i^{2}-2t\,a_i b_i+t^{2}b_i^{2},
\]
and summing over \(i\) gives
\begin{align*}
f(t)&=\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_i^{2}\Bigr)t^{2}
      -2\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i b_i\Bigr)t
      +\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i^{2}\Bigr).
\end{align*}
Write
\[
A:=\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_i^{2},\qquad
B:=-2\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i b_i,\qquad
C:=\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i^{2},
\]
so that \(f(t)=A t^{2}+B t+C\). Clearly \(A\ge 0\).

\textbf{Discriminant condition.}
A quadratic polynomial \(A t^{2}+B t+C\) with \(A\ge 0\) that satisfies (1) for every real \(t\) must have a non‑positive discriminant:
\[
B^{2}-4AC\le 0. \tag{2}
\]

\textbf{From the discriminant to the desired inequality.}
Substituting the expressions for \(A,B,C\) into (2) yields
\begin{align*}
\bigl(-2\!\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i b_i\bigr)^{2}
-4\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_i^{2}\Bigr)\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i^{2}\Bigr)
\le 0.
\end{align*}
Since
\[
(-2S_{ab})^{2}-4S_{b}S_{a}=4\bigl(S_{ab}^{2}-S_{a}S_{b}\bigr),
\]
where \(S_{ab}=\sum a_i b_i\), \(S_{a}=\sum a_i^{2}\) and \(S_{b}=\sum b_i^{2}\), dividing by \(4\) gives
\[
\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i b_i\Bigr)^{2}
\le
\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i^{2}\Bigr)
\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_i^{2}\Bigr).
\]

\textbf{Degenerate cases.}
If \(\sum_{i=1}^{n}b_i^{2}=0\) then each \(b_i=0\), whence the two sides are \(0\). The same argument applies when \(\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_i^{2}=0\).

\textbf{Equality condition.}
Equality in (2) (and hence in the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality) occurs exactly when the discriminant is zero, i.e. when \(B^{2}=4AC\). This is equivalent to the existence of \(t_{0}\in\mathbb{R}\) such that \(f(t_{0})=0\). Because each term in the sum defining \(f\) is non‑negative, \(f(t_{0})=0\) forces \(a_i-t_{0}b_i=0\) for every \(i\); thus there is a constant \(\lambda:=t_{0}\) with \(a_i=\lambda b_i\) for all \(i\). Conversely, if such a \(\lambda\) exists, choosing \(t_{0}=\lambda\) makes \(f(t_{0})=0\) and the discriminant zero, so equality holds. Hence equality holds \emph{iff} the vectors \((a_1,\dots ,a_n)\) and \((b_1,\dots ,b_n)\) are proportional (including the trivial zero‑vector case).
\end{proof}
